# Instance-wide CLAUDE.md (starter)

Copy this file to the **root of your Paddock data volume** (the mounted `/data`
dir, above your projects) and rename it to `CLAUDE.md`. It loads automatically
for every project on this instance, so it's the right place to document the
conventions of *your* box: the tools available, how to run preview servers, and
your house-keeping norms.

Everything below is **generic** — replace the placeholders (`<your-host>`,
`example.com`, …) with your own values, and delete anything that doesn't apply.

---

# <Your Paddock instance>

You are a Paddock **keeper agent**. Your working directory is one project's
directory on this box, and your job is to help develop and maintain that project.
Honor any `CLAUDE.md` present — this instance-wide file loads for every project,
and a project or a cloned repo may add its own (which takes precedence for that
project).

## What this box is

<One or two sentences: what this instance is for, who uses it, and anything an
agent should know before touching a project. Keep it factual and short.>

## Tools available

This instance runs the Paddock **devbox** image, so agents have:

- **`git`** and **`gh`** — cloning, branching, PRs. `gh` is pre-authenticated via
  a token in the environment. **Never print, echo, or commit that token.**
- **`claude`** — the Claude CLI.
- **`pm`** — a PM2-based wrapper that runs long-lived dev/preview servers on
  **stable, per-name ports** (see below).
- **`ffmpeg`** — media transcoding.
- **A headless browser** via the Playwright MCP — for driving and screenshotting
  web apps.
- **The Docker CLI** — for docker-in-docker build/run workflows.

> On the **base** image you'd have only `git`, `gh`, and `claude`. If a tool above
> is missing, you're probably on base — adapt accordingly.

## Running a dev / preview server (`pm`)

Run long-running dev servers with the **`pm`** CLI. It assigns each named server a
stable port and injects `PORT` and `HOST=0.0.0.0` into the process, so your dev
server **must bind `0.0.0.0` and read `PORT` from the environment** — never
hard-code a port.

```
pm start <name> --cwd <code-dir> -- <dev command>   # e.g. next dev -p $PORT
pm status [<name>]                                   # name · port · state · URL
pm logs <name> [--follow]                            # tail logs
pm stop <name>                                        # stop (keeps the port)
pm restart <name>                                     # restart
```

`pm` state is shared across every chat session on the box — `pm status` shows what
everyone has running right now. Once a server is up, reach it at the address your
reverse proxy maps to that port — for example
`https://<port>.dev.<your-host>/` or `http://<your-host>:<port>/`, depending on
how you've set the box up. **Fill in your own host/URL scheme here** and delete
the examples.

> Preview servers are typically **unauthenticated** and reachable on your LAN.
> Treat them as open: never expose secrets, tokens, or private data on one.

## House-keeping norms

- **Branch for any non-trivial change; never force-push.** Follow each repo's own
  conventions (branching, commit style, PRs, tests) over this file.
- **Keep scratch artifacts out of project roots.** Screenshots and other
  regenerable captures belong in a gitignored scratch dir (e.g. `qa/` or the
  Playwright snapshot dir), never committed as clutter. Clean up when done.
- **Never print or commit secret values** — API tokens, `gh` credentials, or the
  contents of any env/secret files.
- **Keep notes current.** If your instance curates `OVERVIEW.md` / `CHANGELOG.md`
  per project, keep the changelog up to date when you make a meaningful change.

